          "kind": "finished",
          "message": "All work is complete and verified. Final summary:\n\n## What I built\n\nA real INI parser (no fixture lookups) in the workspace root, split by responsibility:\n\n| File | Lines | Responsibility |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `scanner.mbt` | 150 | char predicates, cursor helpers, LF/CRLF line splitting, BOM strip, odd/even trailing-backslash continuation joining (reporting `head_len`) |\n| `value.mbt` | 184 | the three carriers (`unquoted`, `\"…\"`, `'…'`), per-carrier escape decoding, quoted/unquoted value parsing, inline-comment scoping |\n| `entry.mbt` | 58 | first `=`/`:` separator, key trimming + validation (non-empty, no control chars, verbatim) |\n| `section.mbt` | 84 | header parsing: first `]`, nested `[` rejected, wrapping-quote stripping, trailing blanks/comment only |\n| `parser.mbt` | 89 | `parse`: line classification, section re-opening, last-wins maps |\n| `printer.mbt` | 83 | `print`: global entries then sections, always double-quoted values, section names quoted when the bare form would lose information |\n\nKey design point: continuation is decided on the **raw** line (odd run = marker, even run = escaped literals), and the joiner reports which characters came from the first physical line, because a continuation line contributes *text*, not syntax — that single fact drives both the inline-comment rule and the \"a quoted value never spans lines\" rule.\n\n## Verification actually run\n\n- `moon test` → **82/82 passing** (12 given public cases incl. both property tests, plus 70 cases I added: dialect fixtures for every spec rule and escape-table row, the four continuation run-lengths, git-config/systemd/php.ini samples, every invalid form, corner-case interactions, print round-trips, a 1000-case extra round-trip property, and deterministic adversarial fuzz + long-chain termination).\n- `moon check` → 0 errors. Two warnings remain, neither in my code: `test_unqualified_package` at `ini_pub_test.mbt:130` (a do-not-modify file) and `unused_package` for the `moonbitlang/core/test` import in the given `moon.pkg`. I deliberately left `moon.pkg` untouched — removing that import could break the withheld private test files if it is not implicitly available to the test package, and the warning is pre-existing in the untouched repo.\n- `moon fmt` applied (clean afterwards), `moon info` regenerated `pkg.generated.mbti`.\n- An independent review subagent audited the code against `specs/ini.md` with ~90 targeted probes and a 40k-input fuzz: no panics, no hangs, spec-faithful on the checkable rules. Its one medium finding — continuation text after an already-closed quoted value was silently discarded — is fixed (such text now raises, since only blanks or a comment may follow the closing quote), with a new test covering it; its dead-assertion finding in the robustness test is also fixed.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- The private suite cannot be run here; the implementation is spec-derived, not fixture-derived, and the review confirmed the continuation-table, escape tables, separator, section and comment rules match the spec text.\n- Judgement calls where the spec is silent are documented in code comments and in `README.md` → \"Design decisions and limits\" (e.g. blanks/comment allowed after a section header's `]`; control characters rejected in keys but not section names; `[\"  \"]` keeps a blank-only name; an inline comment on the first line consumes the line a marker would have joined)."
